];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Can a professional certification really replace an MBA in India?',
    a: 'For a targeted, individual-contributor track — project management, investment research, data analytics, digital marketing, or process improvement — a focused certification paired with real applied proof can get you further, faster, and at a fraction of the cost. It does not replace an MBA for roles that specifically require broad general-management judgment, P&L ownership, or the campus-recruiting pipeline into elite consulting and investment banking. Match the credential to the actual role you are targeting, not to a general sense that "more education is safer."',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is more respected by employers in India: PMP or an MBA?',
    a: 'They answer different questions, so "more respected" depends on the role. For a dedicated project or programme management position in IT, construction, or manufacturing, PMP is a specific, recognised signal that an MBA does not directly replace. For a general management or leadership-track role where cross-functional business judgment matters more than one function, employers weight the MBA, and specifically the institute tier, more heavily.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is CFA a good substitute for an MBA in finance?',
    a: 'Inside investment research, portfolio management, and equity analysis, CFA is often the stronger, more direct signal, and it costs a fraction of a top-tier MBA. It is not a strong substitute if your goal is investment banking associate roles at bulk-bracket banks, which still recruit heavily through top MBA campus pipelines, or if your goal is a broader general-management jump outside finance.',
  },
